WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:07.730
BANJOT CHANANA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:11.230
Welcome to the Cloud
Operations Spotlight.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.270
My name is Banjot
Chanana, I'm one

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:16.720
of the leads for CloudOps
focused on monitoring.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.810
RAMI SHALOM: Hi everyone.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:21.340
My name is Rami Shalom, and I
am a PM Lead in Cloud Operations

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:24.562
focusing on logging and APM.

00:00:24.562 --> 00:00:27.020
Today we're going to talk about
Cloud Operations in Google.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.520
We're going to recap
about everything that we

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.320
have done in the past year.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.670
We're going to demonstrate
the most exciting features

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:37.732
so we'll be able to experience
this more accurately.

00:00:37.732 --> 00:00:39.690
And then we're going to
talk about our roadmap,

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.357
the new exciting
things that we're going

00:00:41.357 --> 00:00:42.840
to work on in the next year.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.180
And some customer highlights,
how they actually use

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.370
the product and [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.610
So what is CloudOps, and
what happened to Stackdriver?

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.285
So we rebranded, and
with this rebranding

00:00:54.285 --> 00:00:56.410
we've done some significant
changes in that product

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.630
to justify this rebranding.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.160
So Cloud Operations
is still a suite

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.290
of products designed to monitor,
troubleshoot, and operate

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.080
your services at scale.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.080
Cloud Operations help developers
and operators-- so you guys--

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.080
keep your applications
fast and available.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.170
The Suite includes
three main products.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:16.520
First one is monitoring.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.730
Monitoring is a framework
to be able to monitor

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:25.280
your metrics, dashboards,
uptime checks, and alerts.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.320
Whereas logging
is a platform that

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.320
allows you to manage your logs,
analyze them, and get the most

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.000
value out of them.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.950
And then we have
traces, which allows

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.570
you to manage your trace.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.550
One more piece to
the mention here

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:41.300
is that those products are
part of a single suite that

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.300
allow you to do a
cross-single correlation.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.190
Banjot, why don't
you tell us about how

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:50.340
scalable this platform is?

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.940
BANJOT CHANANA:
Yeah, folks may be

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.860
asking what makes
us so different from

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.270
every other product out there?

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:59.330
And these products were
born from inside Google.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:02.460
So a lot of the capabilities
you see here today

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.720
were actually developed
for Google by Google.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.540
And what we're doing is
exposing those same capabilities

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.710
to you, our customers.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:13.090
And so the scale in which Google
operates, as you can imagine,

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.320
is quite large.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.480
We've taken that same scalable
back end infrastructure,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.870
and we've now oriented
that to you, our customers.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.290
So we can provide you the
same planet-level scale

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.090
that we provide to
our own websites

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:27.900
to our own capabilities
and our own applications.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.760
Now, some of the stats
that might astound you

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.350
about how much data
we take in today,

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:38.530
50 trillion metric
points in memory

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:43.030
are stored for all the
ingest volume that comes in.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.315
And we've got over 2 and 1/2
exabytes per month of logs

00:02:46.315 --> 00:02:48.280
that enter the system
and that can be analyzed.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.580
So this just gives you an
idea of the type of scale

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.160
that we can operate at.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.170
Now, along with that scale,
what we've been able to do

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:59.530
is ensure that when you
stand out your services

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.450
on Google Cloud,
that you don't have

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.770
to put much effort into
ensuring logging and monitoring.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.440
And advanced
observability capabilities

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.220
are available on the
first day you start out.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:15.460
So there's built in capabilities
for all of your Google Cloud

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:19.780
Services, and zero config needed
to get those up and running.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.850
So that first day that a
new Google Service comes up,

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.840
the next Google Cloud
offering, you'll

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.350
have all the metrics and logs
for all the services available.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.420
As soon as it is GA.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.440
Let's talk a little
bit about what

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.490
we've been doing since
last year at Next to 2019.

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:41.710
We really took a
focus on CloudOps

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:44.140
around three big
areas the first was

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.970
around Enterprise Readiness.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:49.750
We wanted to make sure
that our capabilities allow

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:54.400
for you, our customers,
to comply with all

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.460
of your corporate regulations
and compliance rules that you

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.560
need in order to
make sure it fully

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.570
integrates with the systems
that you already have today.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.680
So whether it's providing
longer term retention

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.920
for logs and metrics, or
whether it's deeper integration

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.590
into third party systems,
we provided a lot

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.060
of the capabilities
that make sure

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:17.290
that you can tie this in to the
systems that you already have,

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.589
and make that transition to
the Cloud that much easier.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:21.787
We focused on ease of use.

00:04:21.787 --> 00:04:23.620
We wanted to make sure
that the products are

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:27.340
easy and simple to use
for even non-power users.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.000
For those that are using
logs and metrics and traces

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.212
for the first time.

00:04:32.212 --> 00:04:33.670
And so you'll see
a lot of the work

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.250
we've done to simplify
the user interface,

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:39.490
and to make it easier to get
to the root cause of what's

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.030
going on in your systems.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.720
And lastly, we focused on GCP.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.730
We wanted to make sure that
every service in GCPs--

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:48.820
all the logs and
the metrics that

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:50.650
are necessary to
troubleshoot what's

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.940
going on with the infrastructure
that you know align--

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.820
are available to you on day one.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:57.500
And so we deeply
integrated with all

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:02.020
of the GCP services from
BigQuery to Google Compute

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:05.950
Engine to GKE or
Kubernetes Engine

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:07.390
and a variety of other services.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:12.630
So on day zero all of
those are ready for you.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.810
Let's get one of
those integrations

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.330
we did for ease of use.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:20.300
We took the entire
Cloud Monitoring Console

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.790
that was previously
in a separate UI

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.700
and a separate browser--

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.740
we brought that into the
Google Cloud Console.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.950
In the same context as all
the other services we use.

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:34.530
Compute Engine BigQuery, GKE.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:39.390
With that in-context capability,
you now have full visibility

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.940
to all of your metrics,
and we improved

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.780
a number of the interfaces
around Cloud Monitoring

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.780
so it's easier to get to your
dashboards, to your alerts,

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:52.040
and to your uptime checks
from one single place.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.270
And you can very easily
then switch back and forth

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:57.300
between Cloud Monitoring and all
the rest of your Google Cloud

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.718
Services.

00:05:58.718 --> 00:06:00.260
Rami, why don't you
talk a little bit

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.005
about some of the work you
did on Log Viewer as well.

00:06:03.005 --> 00:06:04.130
RAMI SHALOM: Thanks Banjot.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:07.190
Similar to what we have
done on the monitoring side,

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:10.340
we have invested heavily
in the Log Viewer.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.490
We actually rebuilt the
product from the ground up

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:16.470
to optimize it for performance
and responsiveness.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.460
So your experience
with the new Log Viewer

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:23.090
should be way better
and easier to work with.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.600
We also invested in
some key components

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:28.580
that will allow you to
better analyze your data.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:30.890
Get the essence of what you
are experiencing and will

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:34.570
be looking for in your
logs directly from the UI

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:35.620
that you have there.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.120
So we invested in
the histogram that

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.260
allows you to see the time
distribution of your logs.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.010
So we'd be able to see
exactly when, there

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.640
is a spike of errors.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:45.730
We have invested in
the Log Field Explorer

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:48.280
to allow you to see
aggregation of your logs

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:49.340
based on field values.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:51.340
So this gives you a
highlight view of everything

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:52.600
that happens there.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:54.620
We have invested in
how streaming operates.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:56.530
We're going to talk
about this a bit later.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.270
We've invested in a
query library that

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:00.430
will allow you to
manage your queries,

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:02.770
because you want to repeat
your work sometimes and get

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:04.573
some recommendations about that.

00:07:04.573 --> 00:07:06.240
And we have been a
bunch of other things

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:09.450
we're going to talk
about in depth.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.700
Other than that, we also
created the New Log Dashboard.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:15.480
This is a great
example of how we

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.220
do the integration between
monitoring, logging,

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:19.350
and traces.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.170
In this case, it's
producing a dashboard

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:26.160
that is focusing on logs
inside the logging UI,

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.320
but relying on the metrics that
happen in Cloud Monitoring.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:35.320
Banjot, why don't you tell
us about SLO monitoring?

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:36.820
BANJOT CHANANA:
Yeah, SLO monitoring

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:40.630
is a great new feature we've
added to really help you reduce

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:42.980
the signal to noise ratio.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.950
So as you add more
and more signals--

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:50.648
whether it's metrics and logs
from your existing workloads--

00:07:50.648 --> 00:07:52.690
it gets harder and harder
to really find the root

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.140
cause of what's happening.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.780
And really what we
want to do is ensure

00:07:55.780 --> 00:08:00.160
that our services are being
measured by how our users are

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:01.990
experiencing those services.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:07.190
Service level objectives is
focused on doing exactly that.

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:09.880
It's ensuring that you look
at the same measurements

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:13.120
and experience it the
same way as your users do,

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.480
by automatically ingesting
the services that

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:20.780
are created on your
GKE-deployed applications.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:24.250
Whether it be through GKE
Labels and Namespaces,

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:28.300
or if you're using our advanced
capabilities with Istio

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:30.490
and Anthos Service
Mesh, we automatically

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.059
find those services.

00:08:32.059 --> 00:08:35.140
And then you can quickly create
availability and performance

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.770
metrics and collect those
to create your service level

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:40.870
indicators, to
which you can then

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:43.419
apply an objective
and a threshold

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:45.040
that you can alert on.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:47.170
What this also does
is allows you to see

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:49.400
a single view of that service.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:50.980
So you can see all
the entities that

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.890
compose that service
and all the signals

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:55.550
related to those entities.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:58.900
The logs, the metrics, and
the traces all in one place.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.760
And we give you that
single pane of glass

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.090
to ensure that you can
troubleshoot that service

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.980
and quickly get to a
resolution on any issue

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:07.654
that you might be experiencing.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:13.190
Now, we've also made this
easier for our system

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:15.860
administrators and
our IT operators, who

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:17.870
have to go through
and instrument

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:21.350
all of their applications
and all of their VMs

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:23.600
to ensure they can
collect those metrics.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:27.560
We've provided this new
Ops Config Agent where

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:31.520
you can define the automation
lifecycle for this agent

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.280
itself, and ensure
that the right VMs get

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:36.770
the right agents with
the right versions,

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:38.330
and they're
automatically installed.

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:41.210
And that policy is
enforced throughout all

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:42.750
your VM instances.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:45.380
And you see a sample
policy command here

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:48.410
that defines which VMs--

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:50.180
in this case all VMs--

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:54.260
get installed agents that
are particular to the logging

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:55.760
and monitoring agents--

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.260
the versions of those agents--

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.900
and you can decide whether
those agents automatically

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:02.070
upgrade when new
versions are available.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.620
And then lastly,
for our power users,

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.690
we added the new
Metrics Query Language.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:12.830
This gives users who are
really taking their metrics

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:16.430
to a new level and looking
to do much more complicated

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:18.650
alerting and
mathematical operations

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:20.150
on times series data--

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:23.630
provides them the ability
to perform those operations

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:27.350
by doing things like allowing
you to do ratios and time

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:28.370
shift on your data.

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:31.490
To do month over month or
year over year analysis.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:34.590
Or in the case of ratios,
to be able to define

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:37.790
a certain new service level
indicators for your service.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:40.160
It allows for complex
grouping and filtering,

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:43.220
so you can take that
data and pivot it

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:45.020
in a number of different
ways, depending

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:48.140
on the metric labels you have
and the types of resources

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:49.710
that you're looking at.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.920
So this really
enables power users

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:55.650
to find what they need within
the time series data they get.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:58.130
And the more data you
collect, the easier

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:01.637
it is to use the Metrics Query
Language to look and sift

00:11:01.637 --> 00:11:03.720
through that data to find
what you're looking for.

00:11:05.978 --> 00:11:06.770
RAMI SHALOM: Right.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:12.070
We've also invested in the power
users in the logging framework.

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:15.910
One notable feature-- maybe
looking tiny but quite

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:18.700
requested by our
customers, by you guys--

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:21.590
is the ability to use regular
expressions within the log

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:22.090
queries.

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:25.510
So today it's available, You are
able to use regular expressions

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:28.600
to identify patterns and
make your queries more

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:31.300
specific and more efficient
to find exactly what you're

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:32.410
looking for.

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:33.910
And we have seen
tremendous traction

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:37.050
with customers
using that feature.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:39.030
Other than that,
many of you have

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.310
been asking us to add the
streaming feature into the Log

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:42.810
Viewer.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.880
So we didn't just do that.

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:48.070
We actually decided to
invest in it further.

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:51.060
So we created a new
API in the product

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.490
that allows you to get
a much better latency.

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:56.230
From 13 seconds
waiting for logs,

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:58.650
now you're going to wait
for less than two seconds.

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.410
The way it's done is
that we have shortcut

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:03.390
at some of the steps along
the ingestion pipeline

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:05.395
logs, and get them
straight to the UI

00:12:05.395 --> 00:12:07.020
for you to be able
to ingest those logs

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.268
and see them right in the UI.

00:12:08.268 --> 00:12:09.810
So you're not going
to wait for them.

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:11.268
When you're running
your code, it's

00:12:11.268 --> 00:12:16.010
going to appear as soon as
the application generates it.

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:18.230
The next one is Log Buckets.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:19.970
One of the key
features that customers

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:23.690
have been asking us is to manage
the logs in a way that fits

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:25.490
their needs in a better way.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:28.610
More specifically, Log
Buckets is a new contract

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:30.680
that allows you to store
the logs, first of all

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:31.747
in a centralized fashion.

00:12:31.747 --> 00:12:33.830
You can collect all the
logs from all the projects

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:36.560
that you have and put them
in one bucket if you wish,

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:40.460
in a way that will allow
you to centralize your logs.

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.470
However, Buckets is also allow
you to do the other way around,

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:46.280
which is basically
decentralize everything.

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:49.040
You may want to have a
bucket or a scope of logs

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:50.820
dedicated for application.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:53.120
So Log Buckets are going to
give you that enablement.

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:56.150
But other than
that, Log Buckets,

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:59.120
as a physical contract,
can be stored regionally.

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:02.510
Which means you can decide that
the certain bucket is not going

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:04.160
to leave a certain region.

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:06.620
That's good for compliance
purposes in some regions,

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:10.130
but it also is useful for
the case of redundancy.

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:13.460
You may want to have full
tolerance between two

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:14.230
different regions.

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:17.900
So you can put the same data
in two different buckets,

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.450
and store it in two
different regions.

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:22.340
The last piece is
that the Log Buckets

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:25.490
will allow us also to offer
you granular permissions

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:26.460
for those buckets.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:30.080
So you'd be able to decide
which users can access which

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:31.970
logs assigned to which bucket.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.270
And we actually intend
to do more than that.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:37.040
We're going to introduce the
concept of Log Views, which

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:39.020
will allow you to get
even more granular access

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:44.530
control in the logs in
each and every bucket.

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:48.400
The last piece I'm going
to mention related to APM

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:52.570
is cross-signal coordination
across traces, metrics,

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:53.950
and logs.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:56.920
Right now, you'll be able
to see that we are going

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:58.450
to be able to access metrics--

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:03.520
sorry, prices-- right from
your monitoring dashboards

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:05.920
or right from your logs
whenever they are available.

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:09.200
And you're going to get a
sense that this is actually

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:09.700
available.

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:12.117
You're going to get the signal
that the trace is available

00:14:12.117 --> 00:14:15.140
and you can click on it and
see exactly the waterfall

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:19.990
of the latency that happens
inside your application.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:22.750
And with that, why don't
we go through a quick demo

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:26.485
to show the main features that
we are doing in the products.

00:14:26.485 --> 00:14:27.940
Welcome everybody.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.790
In this demo, we will
walk you through the new

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:32.830
and the most exciting
features in Google Cloud

00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:37.160
Operations we have been
working on for the past year.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:39.520
As you will see, we
have been working hard

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:42.160
to improve your experience
and productivity,

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:45.620
as you'll observe and
troubleshoot your applications.

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:49.150
We will start our journey
in Cloud Monitoring,

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:53.020
and we'll focus our
attention on services.

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:56.210
In this page, you will
notice a range of services.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:59.440
Some of them are automatically
identified by the systems,

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:01.165
and others were
defined by the users.

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:06.880
Let's focus our attention
on those services

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.610
that look like that could
deserve some attention.

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:13.300
More specifically, let's focus
our attention on Ad Service.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.910
This service appears to be
in distress in the sense

00:15:15.910 --> 00:15:19.290
that it's not meeting our
service-level objective.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:22.900
And we want to take a closer
look at what's happening.

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:27.400
At the top of the page, you
will notice the alert timeline

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:28.690
component.

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:31.840
This component allows you to
review all the alerts that

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:35.500
have been fired for this
service, along this timeline,

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:37.630
and drill down as needed.

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:42.400
Below the alert timeline, you
will notice the SLO component.

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.750
This component
allows you to review

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:47.480
SLOs that have been
defined for this service.

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:50.320
It also allows you
to create a new SLO

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.480
by choosing the
metrics of your choice

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:55.710
as well as the criteria
of your choice.

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:57.950
In this case, we can
notice that the service--

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:01.460
the ad service-- is out
of our budget, which means

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:02.710
it deserves our attention.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:07.210
And we need to drill down
further to see what's going on.

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:10.010
Below the SLO component, you
will notice the key metrics

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:10.990
component.

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:13.240
This component presents
to you the metrics that

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:15.790
are collected for this service.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:18.430
You can review those
metrics at multiple levels

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.470
depending on what
you are looking for.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:23.170
You can do it at the
container, at the pod,

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:25.740
or at the node level.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:29.910
These key metrics can
offer valuable insights

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:32.530
about what may be wrong
with your service.

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:34.230
So that could be a
good starting point

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:38.820
to further explore or
troubleshoot your service.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:43.210
Below the metrics widget, you
will notice the logs widget.

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:45.600
This widget allows
you to explore

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:48.630
the logs that are produced by
the service, which can provide

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:51.420
valuable context
related to every issue

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:54.540
that this service
is experiencing.

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:59.050
For greater details, and
deeper analysis of those logs,

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:03.590
you can choose to open the
logs in the Log Viewer.

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:05.630
We will begin our
journey in Cloud Logging

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:08.270
by presenting the key
components on the page.

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:11.960
The first one is the Log Viewer,
or where the logs are actually

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.040
presented.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:15.194
This is where you will
see all the logs that

00:17:15.194 --> 00:17:16.819
are collected by the
system, and you'll

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:19.099
be able to browse through that.

00:17:19.099 --> 00:17:21.859
You will then notice the
histogram, which gives you

00:17:21.859 --> 00:17:25.790
a time-based distribution of the
logs that are presented below.

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.490
Lastly, there is the
Log Field Explorer.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:31.910
This widget presents to
you an aggregation view

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:34.610
based on field values
in the logs that

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:39.010
appear in the log widget.

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:41.710
To begin our
troubleshooting journey,

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:44.500
we will start with
selecting the resource that

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:47.473
is of interest to us based
on the logs that we see.

00:17:47.473 --> 00:17:49.140
I am interested in
Kubernetes Container,

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:50.470
so I'm going to click that.

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:52.600
And you will notice that
this resource is now

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:54.310
added to the query.

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:56.380
Also, I am interested
in errors that

00:17:56.380 --> 00:17:58.600
are happening in my
application, so I'm

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:00.350
going to click that as well.

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:03.220
And you will notice that
there is a cluster of errors

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:05.620
that appear at
different points in time

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:08.630
along the last six hours.

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:13.670
At this point, we may choose to
narrow the results down further

00:18:13.670 --> 00:18:17.030
based on the time range that
we are interested in the most.

00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:19.730
Or we can simply
focus on the logs

00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:22.880
that appear on screen because
the number is small enough.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:25.760
At this point we can just
get rid of everything

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:27.800
else other than
logs and focus just

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:30.660
on the logs that
appear on screen.

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:35.120
Another option to reach
this narrow set of results

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:39.190
could be via the Log Dashboard.

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:41.210
The Log Dashboard
is a component that

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:43.610
allows you to see an
aggregation of logs

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:47.000
over time, based on specific
resources and specific types

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.230
of logs.

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:50.660
On the left side, you'll
see the overall logs that

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:53.820
are generated in the
resource, on the right side,

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:58.010
you will notice the errors that
are generated for the service.

00:18:58.010 --> 00:19:02.060
This dashboard allows you to
click on one of those bars

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:04.070
and view the logs that
are related to that.

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:08.090
What will happen is that
we'll jump to the Log Viewer

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:10.490
presenting the errors
or the logs that

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:13.970
are related to the specific
bar that I selected.

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:16.140
You've probably noticed
that up to this point,

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:18.920
I didn't interact with
the Query Builder, which

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:23.600
allows me to actually edit the
specific text inside the query

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:26.390
and construct it
the way I want to.

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:29.300
You can further adjust your
query by either editing

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:33.330
the text directly in
the query builder--

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:35.700
and change any of the
fields that appear there--

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:38.730
or use the menus to
construct your query

00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:42.810
based on the resources,
the log name,

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:47.840
or the severity of the logs
that you're interested in.

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:50.030
You'll also notice
a new functionality

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:52.220
that we have added to
manage your queries.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:54.610
Beside the Query
Builder you will notice

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:58.070
a query library that allows you
to review your recent queries

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:01.390
and relaunch them if you
want to, save queries--

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:03.730
queries that you decided
to save and share

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:06.970
with other users in the
project, and suggested queries.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.530
Those are queries
that we actually

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:12.530
suggest to you based on our
understanding of your logs.

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:15.380
Last but not least, I would
like to turn your attention

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:19.190
to the streaming functionality
in the Log Viewer.

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:23.480
This feature allows
you to visualize logs

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:26.300
as they are being generated
by the application.

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:28.760
The delay time is
seconds, which allows

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:30.950
you to troubleshoot your
applications way more

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:33.610
efficiently.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:34.970
Thanks for listening.

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:36.850
We will now go back
to the presentation

00:20:36.850 --> 00:20:39.430
and continue our
discussion focusing

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:41.500
on the roadmap for
Cloud Operations.

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:46.820
BANJOT CHANANA: That was a great
demo Rami, thanks for that.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.840
Let's now talking about what's
coming next for CloudOps.

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:51.400
We've got some
exciting new features

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:54.280
that we think will make it
really easy for our users

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:57.820
to now take advantage of
all the great capabilities

00:20:57.820 --> 00:20:59.800
in our product suite.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:03.070
We'll start with Simplified
Dashboard and Alert Creation.

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:06.220
We find that many users
have a hard time getting

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:08.770
to their first dashboard because
they don't know what metrics

00:21:08.770 --> 00:21:11.800
to use, or they don't quite know
how to visualize those metrics

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:14.210
in a way that makes sense.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:16.690
So we provide a basic
mode that allows

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:21.100
you to get a guided prescriptive
flow to deciding which metrics

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:25.600
to choose using the most
commonly accessed metrics

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:27.760
for your GCP services.

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:30.400
And then providing
recommendations

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:34.570
for how to visualize those
metrics in a chart that makes

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:36.350
sense to you.

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:38.500
We'll also provide the
advanced capabilities

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:43.060
so you can continue to use
the dials and knobs to get all

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:45.400
the right alignment and
grouping and filtering

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:48.040
that you need to, to get
the most out of your data

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:50.770
as you become more
and more advanced

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:55.195
on your usage of the
dashboard capabilities.

00:21:55.195 --> 00:21:57.070
And of course, you'll
have the Query Language

00:21:57.070 --> 00:22:00.520
to do even more complex
queries and arithmetic

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:03.715
operations that you can't do
through the user interface.

00:22:03.715 --> 00:22:05.540
We'll provide a
grid-based layout,

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:08.080
so you can quickly drag
and drop your charts

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:09.210
wherever you want to.

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:11.725
And make sure that right
charts get the right attention

00:22:11.725 --> 00:22:13.520
and they stay in
the right place.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:15.880
Where they can get
sized as big or as small

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:17.380
as you need them to.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:19.580
And we've added
additional chart types,

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:21.340
so you can use
things like heat maps

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:24.490
to find out where
problems are in a very

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:26.530
complicated metric series.

00:22:26.530 --> 00:22:32.560
Or our charts scorecards and
other text-based place holders

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:36.330
to make your charts
work for you.

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:38.240
We're also looking
forward to providing

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:41.000
additional multi-signal
correlation

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.770
using topology information.

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:47.510
Today, we can take a look
at all the microservices

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:51.410
that you've got running in
your GKE cluster as an example,

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:54.140
and map that out in
this topology graph.

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:55.550
That topology
graph will give you

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:59.600
a great sense of the dependency
between your microservices

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:01.100
and which service
is communicating

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:02.620
with which other service.

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:05.390
Now, that's great for
configuration and for looking

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:09.110
at static display, but
what we'd really like to do

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:12.650
is layer on additional
information about latency,

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:14.810
service uptime, and error rates.

00:23:14.810 --> 00:23:16.700
So you can quickly
diagnose and find

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:20.360
the problems that matter
between your microservices.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:22.670
And as it gets more
and more complicated

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:26.400
across your real estate
of your applications,

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.930
we can use the topology graph
to simplify where to look

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:33.400
and how to find the right
problem to solve for.

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:36.618
And then with Proactive
Alerts and Remediation,

00:23:36.618 --> 00:23:38.410
we want to be able to
really get the system

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:42.520
to work for you instead of
just working alongside you.

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:45.340
So with Automated
Alert Creation,

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:47.680
the system will
recommend the types

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:51.260
of alerts you should create for
the GCP services that you have.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:54.700
So on day zero, you have the
best practices configuration

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:55.750
ready to go.

00:23:55.750 --> 00:23:58.030
You can of course,
configure those alerts

00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:00.760
in ways that make sense to
you, but at least you're

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:03.370
provided the recommended
way to look at a service

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:06.330
that you may be looking
at for the first time.

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:08.830
With Log-based Alerts,
you'll be able to quickly get

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:12.250
notifications on events that
happen inside your logs.

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:14.940
So instead of having to
turn those logs into metrics

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:18.340
and then explore them
through the Metrics Explorer,

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:20.890
you can now just
assign alerts based

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:23.320
on patterns and
combinations you see

00:24:23.320 --> 00:24:26.070
in the structure of
the logs themselves.

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:28.390
With Alert
Remediation, we can now

00:24:28.390 --> 00:24:30.540
do typical workflow functions.

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:32.350
So as an alert
fires, wouldn't it

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:34.540
be great if the system
just handled some

00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:36.470
of the common issues for you?

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:37.210
So when a CPU--

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:40.780
when a VM runs out of CPU
or it runs out of disc,

00:24:40.780 --> 00:24:42.970
why not just resize the VM?

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:45.560
If you had bad
deployment in GKE,

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:49.840
and the application is
continuing to service and throw

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:53.920
errors, why not redeploy based
on the last known good state

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:55.300
of that application?

00:24:55.300 --> 00:24:59.290
Or something more complicated
that you've already triaged

00:24:59.290 --> 00:25:01.150
and understand how
to fix, you could

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:03.670
write your own Cloud
Function and execute that

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:05.840
from the alerts themselves.

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.210
And lastly, with
Anomaly-based Alerting,

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:10.510
we really want the
system to work for you.

00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:14.920
And really get ahead of problems
before they become problems.

00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:16.420
So with Anomaly-based
Alerting we'll

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:21.550
threshold or baseline the system
and set automated thresholds

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:25.660
for when we see the system
deviating from normals that we

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:28.350
are viewing year over
year, month over month,

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:29.540
and day over day.

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:32.110
And this is leveraging all
of the great machine learning

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:33.953
capabilities inside of Google.

00:25:33.953 --> 00:25:35.620
Rami, why don't you
tell us a little bit

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:38.230
