the log side of the house?

00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:40.923
RAMI SHALOM: Thanks Banjot,
wow, you have a lot going on.

00:25:40.923 --> 00:25:43.090
Let's talk what's happening
on the Log Insight then.

00:25:43.090 --> 00:25:47.740
So we will continue
the effort of making

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:49.930
the insight, our
logs, more readily

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:51.850
available for you to use.

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:55.780
At this point, it's
more following your lead

00:25:55.780 --> 00:25:59.150
when it comes to what
logs you're looking for.

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:02.320
Our plans forward is to drive
those insights out of the box

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:05.020
without you trying to
figure out what's going on.

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:07.540
So for instance, when
we see a certain scope

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:11.320
of logs in your Log Viewer,
we will service proactively

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:13.662
the trends that are
related to this.

00:26:13.662 --> 00:26:15.370
If there is a bunch
of errors that people

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:17.080
want to be aware
of, or a resource

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:20.050
that is an outstanding resource.

00:26:20.050 --> 00:26:21.880
Or if there is a
field that we see

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:25.570
that is providing the certain
insight that we will want

00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:27.550
to service to you,
those are the types

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:29.830
of things we're
going to show you out

00:26:29.830 --> 00:26:31.840
of the box within
Cloud Logging, using

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:36.750
visual mechanisms to that.

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:39.180
Maybe more importantly,
one of the key investments

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:43.860
that we have right now in Cloud
Logging is our [AUDIO OUT]..

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:47.810
This is a smart
analytics at Cloud-scale,

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:51.150
using SQL relying on BigQuery.

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:52.790
So the idea is that
first of all, we

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:54.620
want to allow you to
have a richer language

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:56.030
to analyze your logs.

00:26:56.030 --> 00:26:59.750
In some cases, you will want
to do more than just filtering

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:00.890
of your logs.

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:03.080
We will want you to do
aggregations, and allow

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:07.040
you to do more sophisticated
operations on your logs.

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:09.020
For that you will need SQL.

00:27:09.020 --> 00:27:11.720
We will use BigQuery
as a back end

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:15.440
to be able to run SQL
operations in Cloud Logging,

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:17.890
in the context, and all the
power and visualizations

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:19.790
that we have in Cloud Logging.

00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:22.550
We will also allow you
to use the same data

00:27:22.550 --> 00:27:26.630
in the logging that is
going to be useful for you

00:27:26.630 --> 00:27:28.205
as part of your
BigQuery analysis.

00:27:28.205 --> 00:27:30.830
So for instance, if you want to
do business analytics involving

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:34.770
multiple repositories of data,
you will be able to do this.

00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:37.520
So combining log data, for
instance, with identities.

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:41.960
Or combining log data with other
parts of your business data

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:44.000
is going to be available
inside BigQuery

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.592
and seamlessly
from Cloud Logging.

00:27:46.592 --> 00:27:48.300
Performance improvements
rely on the fact

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:52.320
that we will be able to use
the BigQuery framework to do

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:55.890
better indexing, and
the analysis of bigger

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:58.260
chunks of data at faster rates.

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:01.190
This is one of the things that
BigQuery is really powerful at,

00:28:01.190 --> 00:28:02.820
and we want to make
sure that we fully

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:07.620
leverage that capability as part
of our Cloud Logging capability

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:09.410
as well.

